there are five major energy sources in  the world right now petroleum natural  gas coal renewable energy and nuclear  power thanks to the US shale and  fracking boom and the fact that it is  now cheap plentiful and clean its  natural gas that may be the fuel of the  future big oil companies are using a  technology that turns it into liquefied  natural gas or LNG a commodity that can  be shipping all over the world reshaping  the politics of global energy so here's  the situation the trickiest problem with  natural gas right now is right there in  the name it's a gas most gas travels  through pipelines that makes it fairly  difficult if the US wants to sell its  gas to say Korea but a few things make  shipping natural gas possible first is  the process of super cooling natural gas  to minus 260 degrees Fahrenheit  converting it to a liquid and reducing  its volume by about 600 times it's like  going from a beach ball to a ping-pong  ball this makes it possible to transport  gas not through pipelines but on really  big ships which leads us to a fleet of  250 million dollar tankers some the size  of almost four American football fields  that can ferry LNG all over the world  the only problem is these ships were too  big to fit through the Panama Canal  until now the five billion dollar Panama  Canal expansion project was finally  finished in June 2016 allowing for  faster shipping of LNG to key markets  like Asia so what does this mean for  global energy well a growing number of  countries are trading in LNG with 34  nations importing it by the end of 2014  compared to just 15 in 2005 now here's  the argument overall demand for LNG  depends on how fast countries will turn  away from coal the largest source of  power generation in the world one LNG  selling point is that it's cleaner  producing about half as much carbon  dioxide as coal oil companies are  pushing into the LNG world to embrace a  greener future Shell's fifty three  billion dollar acquisition of UK oil and  producer BG group the industry's biggest  deal in a decade made the Dutch giant  the world's largest LNG trader but not  everyone's aboard the LNG trade hole is  still cheaper renewables pollute less  and countries like Japan the largest  importer of LNG in the world is focused  on nuclear power after starting to get  its reactors back up and running after  shutdowns prompted by the 2011 tsunami  plus some US gas utilities are opposed  to large-scale exports of LNG saying  that longer term it can raise gas prices  for American consumers
 

